WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.700
I'm making this video at a time distant from any of my books or movie adaptation stuff coming out

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.140
so that it doesn't appear to be about me, which I really hope it isn't.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:13.540
But of course, I'm inevitably warped by my own experience. More on how I might be wrong later, actually.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:16.200
Okay. So I started out my writing career as a book reviewer.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:20.900
Between 2000 and 2005, I reviewed hundreds of books for Booklist magazine.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.400
I reviewed young adult novels, picture books, romance novels,

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.580
books about boxing, books about early Islamic history, and books about conjoined twins.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:33.020
The number of books about conjoined twins, by the way, is truly astonishing.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:36.420
Also, I've been lucky enough to review some great books for the New York Times Book Review

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.600
including Eleanor &amp; Park, The Book Thief, and The Hunger Games.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.360
This all happened back at the beginning of the age of everyone reviewing everything,

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.560
a wave that I have ridden profitably, if at times uncomfortably, my entire adult life.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.360
In fact, my ongoing fascination with the format and the ubiquity of the review led me to my podcast:

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:54.780
The Anthropocene Reviewed.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.220
I was like, "If people can review their haircuts and their breakfasts,

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:01.940
why not cholera or the strange human habit of whispering?"

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.940
So to be clear, I am strongly in favor of reviewing.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.360
I think thoughtful analysis of art is vital to the creation of good art.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.040
I think it can be super helpful to look at art through a lens.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.717
Like looking at Game of Thrones through the lens of climate change, for instance

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.640
or looking at Jane Eyre through the lens of shifting gender norms.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.280
But I tend to be less moved by criticism in the colloquial sense of the word, like articles or videos

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:30.900
explaining "Everything Wrong with X" or "14 Reasons Z is Terrible" or whatever.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:35.180
Now of course, it's important to point out flaws in art. That can help encourage the creation of better art.

00:01:35.189 --> 00:01:38.160
But I think the most important thing criticism can do is

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.520
find and lift up important and beautiful work that might otherwise go unnoticed.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.900
I don't read to discover what's wrong with a story,

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.580
although there are many something's wrong with every story.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.124
I read to be moved, in every sense of the word.

00:01:51.124 --> 00:01:55.630
To go to places I haven't been and to glimpse worlds I otherwise couldn't see,

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:57.820
including the worlds deep within myself.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:02.340
Critics, whether in the New York Times or on Goodreads, can help us to discover the books that will move us, and

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.480
also give us a framework through which to think about them.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:10.880
So I think what we mean when we say a book is "good" is that it's worth our attention.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:15.580
A good book is one that, if read generously, will offer big gifts in return.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:18.800
And part of what frustrates me about the "Everything Wrong with X" school of criticism

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.000
is that it mostly makes the case for not paying attention to something,

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.920
which I think is a case usually best made by not paying attention to something.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:32.140
Of course, there are exceptions. When really popular work is culturally destructive, for instance.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:34.360
But I just don't think that in general,

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:39.480
communities organized around hating art that deserves less attention are as productive as

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:44.160
communities organized around loving art that deserves more attention.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.560
So that's how I try to read in critique:

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:49.640
with an eye toward "What about this experience is valuable and worthy of attention?"

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.800
"What stories have expanded my universe or changed the way I think?"

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.080
Among recent reads,

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.720
Melinda Gates' 'The Moment of Lift' has changed the way I think about gender discrimination and its role in poverty.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.680
The graphic novel adaptation of 'Kindred' has kept me thinking about the past's omnipresence.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:07.020
Both those books felt like really good investments of my attention,

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.600
which is how I'm trying to think about reading these days.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.840
I'm trying to think less about whether something is good or bad and

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.680
more about which works of art I should look at and how to look at them.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.760
Of course, I might be wrong about how to read in review.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:23.040
I do remember a time when the ideals that animated my life felt certain and important,

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.800
and I was absolutely sure that I was contributing to culture in a positive way.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:30.880
But that moment has passed, and now I am uncertain about everything.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.680
I'm even uncertain about being uncertain!

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.560
Should I have unshakable beliefs? Maybe.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.200
But for now, I'm letting books, art, and life shake them.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:42.960
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

